    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=299896aa-9c25-4db1-b4b7-a64f6c01ab51": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant",
      "description": "Graduate Assistant - Campus Engagement & Intramural Programs | Job Opportunities Skip to main content Accessibility Note: If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following page: Accessibility Accommodation for Applicants Dismiss Note Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Graduate Assistant - Campus Engagement & Intramural Programs Job Category : Staff Requisition Number : GRADU001836 Apply now Posting Details Posted : September 8, 2026 Part-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description Position Code: 8009 DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: Office of Student Affairs, Student Campus Life POSITION : Temporary, Non- Exempt (9 months), Part-time (15 Hours) SALARY RANGE: $16.90-$16.90 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. INTRODUCTORY PERIOD: Six (6) Months AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 9 , 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: The Graduate Assistant, Campus Engagement & Intramural Programs supports the mission of the Office of Student Campus Life by creating meaningful opportunities for student involvement, leadership development, recreation, and belonging. Reporting to the Manager of Student Campus Life Activities, this position assists in the planning, implementation, assessment, and day-to-day operations of campus engagement initiatives, with primary responsibility for intramural sports, support for recognized student clubs and organizations, student engagement technology, and commuter student engagement. The Graduate Assistant serves as a key student leader within Student Campus Life, collaborating with campus partners, student organizations, and student employees to create inclusive, engaging, and educational co-curricular experiences that contribute to student success and a vibrant campus community. This position requires flexible work hours, including evenings, weekends, and occasional holidays to support student programming and events. Duties and Responsibilities: Under the direction of the Manager of Campus Life Activities, responsibilities include but are not limited to: Intramural Programming • Manage the IMLeagues platform, including registrations, scheduling, communications, and league administration. • Recruit, train, schedule, and supervise student officials and scorekeepers. • Plan and implement intramural leagues, tournaments, and special recreational events. • Coordinate facility reservations and equipment needs for intramural activities. • Promote participation through marketing, outreach, and campus engagement initiatives. • Evaluate participation trends and recommend improvements to increase student engagement. Clubs & Organizations • Assist student leaders with organization processes including registration, event planning, risk management, and leadership development. • Provide administrative support for club trainings, workshops, and organizational development initiatives. • Assist with planning and execution of involvement fairs, leadership programs, and other Student Campus Life signature events. • Support club advisors and student leaders by connecting them with appropriate campus resources. Student Engagement Portal • Assist with administration of the Student Engagement Portal (Start@Redlands), including organization registration, event submissions, and content management. • Review and process event requests within established Student Campus Life procedures. • Maintain the Student Campus Life calendar to ensure accurate and timely communication of student programs and events. • Support data collection, assessment, and reporting using engagement platform analytics. Student Commuter Lounge Operations • Supervise and support Student Commuter Lounge Ambassadors, including coordinating schedules, providing task direction during shifts, and serving as a peer leader who reinforces professionalism, customer service, and departmental expectations. • Assist in planning and implementing commuter-focused programs, initiatives, and community building activities in collaboration with Student Campus Life staff. General Student Engagement • Collaborate with campus departments to promote student engagement and co-curricular learning. • Support assessment efforts by collecting participation data and preparing reports that inform program improvement. • Represent Student Campus Life at university events, committee meetings, and outreach opportunities as assigned. • Perform other duties and special projects as assigned. Qualifications Guidelines: Any combination of experience/training/education equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Required • Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution relating to education, project management, sports, recreation. • Demonstrated interest in student affairs, higher education, leadership, recreation, student organizations, campus programming, or related fields. • Experience serving in student leadership, campus employment, volunteer leadership, athletics, recreation, or comparable involvement. • Ability to work evenings and weekends as needed. • Valid California driver's license (or ability to obtain one upon hire) with an acceptable driving record. Preferred • Experience planning programs, events, or recreational activities. • Experience working with student organizations, student government, orientation, recreation, residence life, leadership programs, or similar collegiate experiences. • Experience supervising, mentoring, or leading peers. • Familiarity with campus engagement software (such as IMLeagues, Microsoft Office, or similar platforms). • Experience with social media, marketing, or event promotion. • CPR/First Aid certification (or willingness to obtain). Knowledge and Skills: • Ability to build positive working relationships with students from diverse backgrounds. • Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously. • Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. • Ability to work independently while contributing effectively to a collaborative team. • Strong customer service orientation and commitment to student success. • Ability to exercise sound judgment, professionalism, and discretion. • Experience or willingness to learn student engagement technologies and software platforms. • Ability to analyze participation data and make recommendations for program improvement. • Ability to recruit, supervise, mentor, and develop student employees and student leaders. • Knowledge of leadership development, student engagement, or collegiate recreation principles is preferred. • Commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and creating welcoming campus environments. • Ability to adapt to changing priorities in a fast-paced student affairs environment. • Familiarity with Microsoft Office and Google Workspace applications. Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Standard office and field setting. Duties performed are in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings, and outside at sports recreation facilities. The position requires working some evenings and weekends. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly sit for long periods; stand, and walk on a regular basis; occasionally may run; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; stoop or kneel or crouch to file; speak clearly and distinctly to answer telephones and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 20 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. BACKGROUND CHECK: Employment is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Offers are conditional and may be rescinded if disqualifying information is found or if information was withheld or falsified. Current employees may be affected if the check is not satisfactorily completed. DRIVER’S LICENSE: Certain positions require a valid driver’s license and enrollment in the DMV Government Employer Pull Notice Program to verify license status and driving record. PHYSICAL/PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: Select positions (e.g., Public Safety, Facilities, Student Affairs, Athletics, ITS) require a pre-employment physical exam, including the ability to lift 50+ lbs. Public Safety Officers must also pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. \nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=ceeefa2f-254b-46eb-ae47-fe44fa2e8263": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant",
      "description": "Graduate Assistant - Student Leadership and Engagement Programs | Job Opportunities Skip to main content Accessibility Note: If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following page: Accessibility Accommodation for Applicants Dismiss Note Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Graduate Assistant - Student Leadership and Engagement Programs Job Category : Staff Requisition Number : GRADU001835 Apply now Posting Details Posted : September 8, 2026 Part-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description Position Code: 8008 DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: Office of Student Affairs, Student Campus Life POSITION : Temporary, Non- Exempt (9 months), Part-time (15 Hours) SALARY RANGE: $16.90-$16.90 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. INTRODUCTORY PERIOD: Six (6) Months AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 9 , 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: The Graduate Assistant, Student Leadership and Engagement Programs supports the mission of the office of Student Campus Life by advancing leadership development, student engagement, and campus traditions through innovative programming, student leader advising, and co-curricular learning experiences. Reporting to the Assistant Dean of Leadership and Engagement Programs, the Graduate Assistant serves as an integral member of the Student Campus Life team and assists in the planning, implementation, assessment, and continuous improvement of leadership initiatives, student government, campus programming, orientation experiences, and leadership education. The Graduate Assistant works collaboratively with student leaders, campus departments, faculty, staff and community partners to cultivate meaningful involvement opportunities that foster belonging, leadership development, civic responsibility, and student success. This position requires flexible work hours, including evenings, weekends, and occasional academic break periods to support campus events and student programs. Duties and Responsibilities: The duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following: Under the direction of the Assistant Dean of Leadership and Engagement Programs, responsibilities include but are not limited to: Leadership Development • Assist in the planning, implementation, and assessment of leadership development initiatives for undergraduate students. • Support the development and facilitation of the W.O.O.F. Leadership curriculum, leadership workshops, retreats, and educational experiences. • Assist with leadership training opportunities for student organization officers, student government leaders, orientation leaders, and emerging student leaders. • Support leadership learning outcomes through assessment and continuous improvement practices. Student Government Advising • Assist with advising and supporting the Redlands Student Government (RSG) in achieving organizational goals and initiatives. • Attend weekly RSG meetings, committee meetings, and major events. • Assist with student government elections, onboarding, officer transitions, and leadership development. • Support governance processes including meeting logistics, agenda preparation, communications, and documentation. Campus Programming • Assist with planning and implementation of campus traditions and signature Student Campus Life programs, including DAWGS events, leadership recognition events, Bulldog Beginnings, and other university traditions. • Collaborate with student leaders to develop engaging, inclusive, and educational campus programming. • Coordinate program logistics including space reservations, vendor communication, purchasing, catering, contracts, and event setup. • Assist with marketing and promotional efforts through social media, digital signage, newsletters, and other communication platforms. Student Leadership Support • Serve as a mentor and resource to student leaders, providing guidance on leadership development, organizational effectiveness, event planning, and university policies. • Support leadership recognition initiatives, awards, and appreciation programs. • Assist with leadership coaching and individual student leader development. New Student Orientation • Assist in the planning, implementation, and assessment of New Student Orientation, transfer student programming, and leadership transition experiences. • Support recruitment, training, and supervision of Orientation Leaders and student volunteers as assigned. Administrative & Operational Support • Provide administrative support to the Assistant Dean of Leadership and Engagement Programs. • Assist with calendar management, purchasing, budget tracking, event reservations (25Live), and departmental communications. • Coordinate meeting logistics, maintain program records, and prepare reports. • Assist with assessment efforts by collecting participation data and preparing program evaluations. • Represent Student Campus Life at campus events, committee meetings, and outreach opportunities. • Collaborate with professional staff across Student Affairs to promote student engagement and belonging. Qualifications Guidelines: Any combination of experience/training/education equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Required • Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution. • Demonstrated leadership experience through student organizations, student government, orientation, campus employment, athletics, community service, or comparable involvement. • Strong interest in higher education, student affairs, leadership development, or student engagement. • Ability to work evenings and weekends as programming requires. • Valid California driver's license (or ability to obtain one upon hire) with an acceptable driving record. Preferred • Experience planning campus events or leadership programs. • Experience serving in student government, orientation, residence life, programming boards, leadership organizations, or similar roles. • Experience facilitating presentations, workshops, or leadership development activities. • Experience advising, mentoring, or supervising peers or student employees. • CPR/First Aid certification (preferred but not required). Knowledge and Skills: • Strong organizational and project management skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities. • Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. • Ability to establish positive relationships with students, faculty, staff, and campus partners. • Ability to mentor, coach, and develop student leaders. • Strong customer service orientation and commitment to student success. • Ability to facilitate workshops, presentations, and leadership development experiences. • Ability to work collaboratively within a team while exercising sound independent judgment. • Experience or willingness to learn student engagement technologies, Microsoft Office, Google Workspace, and university software platforms. • Ability to assess programs, interpret participation data, and recommend improvements. • Commitment to fostering inclusive, welcoming, and student-centered environments. • Flexibility to adapt to changing priorities in a dynamic student affairs environment. • Professionalism, initiative, creativity, and attention to detail. • Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise discretion when working with student information. Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Standard office and field setting. Duties performed are in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings, and outside at event venues. The position requires working some evenings and weekends. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly sit for long periods; stand, and walk on a regular basis; occasionally may run; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; stoop or kneel or crouch to file; speak clearly and distinctly to answer telephones and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 20 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. BACKGROUND CHECK: Employment is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Offers are conditional and may be rescinded if disqualifying information is found or if information was withheld or falsified. Current employees may be affected if the check is not satisfactorily completed. DRIVER’S LICENSE: Certain positions require a valid driver’s license and enrollment in the DMV Government Employer Pull Notice Program to verify license status and driving record. PHYSICAL/PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: Select positions (e.g., Public Safety, Facilities, Student Affairs, Athletics, ITS) require a pre-employment physical exam, including the ability to lift 50+ lbs. Public Safety Officers must also pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. \nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=ba4c495b-976a-40ca-8c94-082549b0321f": {
      "title": "Public Safety Officer, Part-time",
      "description": "Public Safety Officer, Part-time | Job Opportunities Skip to main content Accessibility Note: If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following page: Accessibility Accommodation for Applicants Dismiss Note Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Public Safety Officer, Part-time Job Category : Staff Requisition Number : PUBLI001840 Apply now Posting Details Posted : September 15, 2026 Part-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations Travel Required : Yes Job Details Description POSITION CODE: 8000 DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: Public Safety POSITION : Staff, Non-Exempt, Part-time (20 Hours) SALARY RANGE: $23.42 Per Hour INTRODUCTORY PERIOD: Six (6) Months AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 15, 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: The Public Safety Officer performs a wide variety of duties to provide security and protection over persons and buildings and/or properties within the college community while ensuring a year-round, safe campus environment including patrolling, and securing buildings, grounds, and other College property, enforcing campus rules and regulations. Reporting to the Public Safety Sergeant, this serves as a first responder and liaison to local authorities, including the City of Redlands Police and Fire Departments. Public Safety Officers take an active role in areas of public education relative to crimes and crime prevention. The Public Safety Department is a 24/7 operation. Duties and Responsibilities: The duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following: Maintains general campus security by patrolling and providing direction on initial response to calls for service, emergencies, and criminal situations; investigates and documents incidents; responds to safety hazards, fires, medical and/or criminal emergencies; provides emergency first aid and assists in the transportation of ill or injured persons; logs and reports activities. Directs traffic and provides crowd control at large gatherings and events; escorts employees, students and visitors; patrols the campus on foot, by bicycle or vehicle; answers calls to protect persons and property; provides assistance to access to campus buildings and secures them; transports students and/or faculty; participates in crime prevention programs; conducts safety training for students including fire drills, evacuation procedures, alcohol and drug abuse. Conducts investigations of reported crimes, traffic accidents, and other incidents occurring on university property; identifies suspicious situations; works with outside agency contacts; acts as deterrent to crime and vandalism; observes unsafe conditions, safety hazards, and reports them to proper department for correction. Prepares verbal and written administrative reports related to incidents and/or arrests; gathers pertinent information from participants or witnesses according to established regulations and guidelines; and documents all shift activity. Warns violators of rule infractions such as parking, loitering, fighting, or carrying forbidden articles and apprehends or expels persons engaging in suspicious or criminal acts; enforces college regulations and the law; acts as a liaison between university community, Police Department, Fire Department, and other agencies. Perform other duties and special projects as assigned. Qualification Guidelines Any combination equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Experience/Training/Education: Required High school diploma or equivalent is required. One year of experience in law enforcement or a closely related field required. A valid California Class C CA drivers’ license with a good driving record is required. Note: Out-of-state licenses are valid until appointment. A valid California Class “C” driver’s license must be obtained within ten (10) days of appointment (in accordance with CA Vehicle Code 12505c). A physical examination demonstrating physical ability to perform the essential functions of the job, a psychological evaluation, and a comprehensive background check are required. Special License/Certification: Possession of a Basic POST Certificate or higher OR a valid Level 1 Reserve Officer POST Certificate is Possession of a PC 832 course is required. Courses taken prior to 2007, the basic course requirement shall be the course(s) that were prescribed at the time of training required by Section 832 and any other training prescribed by the commission. A California Guard Card with Baton permit is required within 90 days of hire or appointment. CPR, AED, and First Aid Certificates must be obtained within 90 days of hire or appointment. Note: Copies of pertinent licenses and certifications will be required. Knowledge and Skills: Knowledge of applicable federal and state laws and regulations. Interpret, apply, explain, and ensure compliance with applicable Federal, State, and local policies, procedures, laws, and regulations. Comprehensive knowledge and understanding of concepts, principles, and practices of public safety. Thorough knowledge of California criminal law and vehicle codes. Considerable independent judgment and initiative. Ability to remain calm and understanding in stressful situations exercised in interpreting orders, rules, and regulations in emergency situations with the public and fellow employees. Excellent verbal and written communication skills to effectively communicate with diverse populations. Proficient computer skills using Microsoft Office Suite including Word and Outlook. Ability to operate standard office equipment. Prepare clear and accurate reports, records, and other written materials. Ability to supervise and evaluate employees effectively. Safety practices and equipment related to the work. Operate a motor vehicle in a safe manner under patrol conditions. Techniques of first aid and CPR. Knowledge of use and care of radio equipment, telephone, computer, Fire-Access – CCTV monitoring systems and other technical equipment. Must be able to competently interact with a culturally and ethnically diverse population of students, faculty, and staff. Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Field and standard office setting. Duties performed in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings. At least minimal environmental controls to assure health and comfort. The position may require after-hours and emergency response. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly works outdoors; walk for long periods of time; climbs hills and stairs; travels to various locations; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; frequently bends, stoops, kneels, reaches to perform work; climb stairs; speak clearly and distinctly to answer telephones and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 100 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. The University of Redlands is required to provide a reasonable estimate of the compensation range for this role. This range considers the wide range of factors that are considered in making compensation decisions including but not limited to experience, skills, knowledge, abilities, education, licensure and certifications, and other business and organizational needs. It is not typical for an individual to be offered a salary at or near the top of the range for a position. Salary offers are determined based on final candidate qualifications and experience. The full salary range for this position is defined as the Full Pay Range. The budgeted salary or hourly range that the University reasonably expects to pay for this position is defined as the Hiring Range . Placement within the Hiring Range is determined by internal equity, and relevant qualifications. BACKGROUND CHECK: Satisfactory completion of a background check (including a criminal records check) is required for employment. The University will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background check reveals disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background check may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position. DRIVER'S LICENSE CHECK: Possession of a valid Driver's License is required for certain positions. Employees in positions that require a valid Driver’s License will be enrolled in the Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Government Employer Pull Notice Program which confirms possession of a valid driver's license and reflects driving record. PHYSICAL AND PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: For certain positions typically in Public Safety, Facilities, Athletics and Information Technology Services departments, employment is contingent upon a candidate passing a pre-employment physical exam with the ability to lift in excess of 50 lbs. Public Safety Officers, in addition to a pre-employment physical exam, will also be required to pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Travel Required Yes . A valid California Class C CA drivers’ license with a good driving record. Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. \nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
