      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "OpportunityDetail.Index.PageTitle Layout.AccessibilityBanner.Header Layout.AccessibilityBanner.Message Layout.AccessibilityBanner.LinkMessage Layout.AccessibilityBanner.Dismiss U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Common.Post Administrative Assistant Opportunity.Opportunities.JobCategory : Staff Opportunity.Opportunities.RequisitionNumber : ADMIN001844 Opportunity.Create.Publishing.ThirdPartyJobBoards.MoreInfoModal.Title Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.PostedLabel : September 17, 2026 Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.FullTime Common.JobLocationType OpportunityDetail.CompanyInformation.Locations California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA Opportunity.Opportunities.MoreJobLocations Opportunity.Opportunities.LessJobLocations Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.JobDetails Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.Description DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: Office of Student Success Center POSITION : Full-Time , Non-Exempt (Twelve months), Full-time (40 Hours) SALARY RANGE: $17.00 - $21.80 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 17 , 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: The Administrative Assistant – Support Services provides administrative and operational support for Disability & Accessibility Services (DAS), Counseling Services, and the Office of Equity & Title IX through a shared administrative support model. The position serves as the primary front desk representative for these offices and coordinates daily administrative operations, scheduling, confidential records, communications, and office logistics while providing excellent customer service to students, faculty, staff, families, and visitors. The position works collaboratively with professional staff to support the delivery of services while maintaining strict confidentiality and ensuring efficient office operations.  The position also provides occasional administrative support for the SSC for shared administrative needs, including for the University of Redlands Los Angeles SSC support as needed. Duties and Responsibilities: The duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following: Shared Administrative Operations • Coordinate daily office operations. • Provide front desk reception and customer service. • Answer phones, respond to inquiries, and direct visitors appropriately. • Schedule appointments, meetings, and events. • Maintain calendars and shared schedules. • Prepare routine correspondence, reports, and forms. • Order supplies and coordinate purchasing. • Maintain departmental records and filing systems. • Assist with departmental communications. • Coordinate meetings and special events. Disability & Accessibility Services Provide administrative support for Disability & Accessibility Services, including: • Maintaining confidential student records. • Assisting with the scheduling of student appointments when needed. • Coordinating accommodated testing schedules and logistics. • Conducting proctoring of accommodated examinations. • Coordinating transportation accommodations, including managing the schedule for golf cart transportation and operating the department golf cart. • Assisting with assistive technology and equipment support and logistics. • Supporting outreach, educational programming, and events. • Communicating with students, faculty, staff, visitors, and families as needed. • General administrative support to the Director of Disability and Accessibility Services. • Other administrative duties as assigned. Counseling Services Provide administrative support by: • Assisting with the scheduling of student appointments when needed. • Maintaining and organizing records. • Supporting office operations. • Assisting with communications. • Coordinating workshops and outreach activities. • Handling walk-in students and triaging crisis situations to appropriate personnel as needed. • General administrative support to the Director of Counseling Services • Other administrative duties as assigned. Office of Equity & Title IX Provide administrative support by: • Scheduling meetings and hearings. • Organizing confidential records. • Preparing administrative and hearing materials, evidence packets and other documents. • Coordinating educational programming or training. • Supporting office communications and operations. • General administrative support to the Director of the Office of Equity and Title IX Coordinator. • Other administrative duties as assigned. Shared Student Success Center Support • Provide limited administrative support for shared Student Success Center operations, including purchasing, office logistics, tutoring support, departmental meetings, and student employee administrative processes. • Provide general administrative support to the Associate Dean of Student Success and Accessibility. • Provide support for Student Affairs events and programs, admissions and recruitment events, new student orientation, and other institution-wide initiatives, events, and/or programming. Qualification Guidelines Any combination equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Experience/Training/Education: Required Associate degree required; bachelor's degree preferred. A minimum of three years of administrative support experience, preferably in an education context. Excellent organizational and communication skills. Demonstrated ability to manage confidential information. Proficiency with Microsoft Office and standard office technology. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education. Experience supporting student services or compliance-related offices. Experience with student information or case management systems. Knowledge and Skills: Excellent interpersonal and customer service skills. Strong organizational and time-management abilities. Ability to manage multiple priorities. Ability to maintain confidentiality. Ability to work collaboratively with diverse populations. Attention to detail and accuracy. Ability to learn new technologies and software systems. Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Standard office setting. Duties performed are primarily in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings. At least minimal environmental controls to assure health and comfort. Must be willing and able to travel and to work non-traditional hours including nights and weekends, as required. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly sit for long periods; walk short distances on a regular basis; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; stoop or kneel or crouch to file; climb stairs; speak clearly and distinctly to answer telephones and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 20 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. The University of Redlands is required to provide a reasonable estimate of the compensation range for this role. This range considers the wide range of factors that are considered in making compensation decisions including but not limited to experience, skills, knowledge, abilities, education, licensure and certifications, and other business and organizational needs. It is not typical for an individual to be offered a salary at or near the top of the range for a position. Salary offers are determined based on final candidate qualifications and experience. The full salary range for this position is defined as the Full Pay Range. The budgeted salary or hourly range that the University reasonably expects to pay for this position is defined as the Hiring Range . Placement within the Hiring Range is determined by internal equity, and relevant qualifications. BACKGROUND CHECK: Satisfactory completion of a background check (including a criminal records check) is required for employment. The University will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background check reveals disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background check may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position. DRIVER'S LICENSE CHECK: Possession of a valid Driver's License is required for certain positions. Employees in positions that require a valid Driver’s License will be enrolled in the Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Government Employer Pull Notice Program which confirms possession of a valid driver's license and reflects driving record. PHYSICAL AND PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: For certain positions typically in Public Safety, Facilities, Athletics and Information Technology Services departments, employment is contingent upon a candidate passing a pre-employment physical exam with the ability to lift in excess of 50 lbs. Public Safety Officers, in addition to a pre-employment physical exam, will also be required to pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.Qualifications Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.Skills Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.Behaviors : Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.Motivations : Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.Education.Header Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.Experience Opportunity.OpportunityDetail.LicensesAndCerts Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. \nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=615431b6-cd0e-43f2-89d6-d53564631f9d": {
      "title": "Academic Operations Specialist",
      "description": "Academic Operations Specialist | Job Opportunities Skip to main content Accessibility Note: If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following page: Accessibility Accommodation for Applicants Dismiss Note Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Academic Operations Specialist Job Category : Staff Requisition Number : ACADE001847 Apply now Posting Details Posted : September 22, 2026 Full-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description Position Code: 8021 DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: College of Arts and Sciences POSITION : Staff, Non- Exempt (12 months), Full-time (40 Hours) SALARY RANGE: $17.00-$25.00 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. INTRODUCTORY PERIOD: Six (6) Months AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 22 , 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: The Academic Operations Specialist supports the academic operations of the College of Arts and Sciences for the Office of the Dean. Working under the direction of the Associate Deans, the Specialist coordinates cross-unit processes involving admissions, the registrar, academic departments, and student-facing offices; administers and maintains the systems and documents that support new-student registration, advising, and curricular scheduling; and exercises independent judgment in prioritizing and coordinating these processes. The position also provides project support to the Associate Deans for academic and faculty programming including the Core Four initiative and the Center for Immersive and Experiential Learning Opportunities (CIELO). The Specialist serves a College-wide coordinating function within the Dean’s Office and is distinguished from department-level coordination by its cross-unit scope and its independent-judgment responsibilities. Duties and Responsibilities: The duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following: New-Student Academic Onboarding and Registration Operations Coordinate the academic operations surrounding the onboarding and registration of entering students, including both first-year and transfer populations. Coordinate transfer student intake, including the assignment of transfer students to faculty advisors, follow-up on transcript evaluation, and coordination among admissions, the registrar, and academic departments to move admitted transfer students through advising and registration. Maintain accurate records and tracking of new student academic checklist items. Contribute to the design and implementation of revised new-student onboarding and registration processes, applying operational knowledge to support process improvement. Curricular Operations Coordination Support the Associate Dean for Curricular Affairs in course enrollment monitoring and related departmental petitions and maintaining accurate records of decisions. Maintain curricular and advising documents and materials, including syllabus statements, advising handouts, and related catalog-adjacent materials, ensuring they are current and consistent. Coordinate and track other recurring curricular operations processes as assigned, exercising independent judgment in prioritization and flagging non-routine matters for the Associate Dean of Curricular Affairs. Project Support to the Associate Deans Provide coordination and logistical support for academic programming, including major/minor fair, academic open houses, the annual CIELO Symposium, and similar College events. Provide coordination and logistical support for faculty programming, including faculty writing retreats, professional development sessions, and related events. Support other time-limited projects and initiatives of the Office of the Dean as assigned by the Associate Deans. General Communicate and collaborate effectively with academic departments, admissions, the registrar, student-facing offices, and other University units to support coordinated academic operations. Maintain the confidentiality of student records and exercise sound professional judgment in handling sensitive information. Engage in professional development and maintain current knowledge relevant to academic operations, registration, and advising support. Perform other job-related duties as reasonably assigned by the Dean or Associate Deans. Qualifications Guidelines: Any combination of experience/training/education equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Required Bachelor’s degree required. A minimum of two (2) years of experience in a higher education setting, preferably in academic operations, registration, records, advising support, admissions, or a related student-services or academic-affairs function. Experience coordinating processes that involve multiple offices or stakeholders preferred. Knowledge and Skills: Excellent organizational skills and detail orientation with high accuracy. Ability to prioritize competing tasks, meet deadlines, and work independently with minimal supervision in a fast-paced environment with cyclical peaks and changing priorities. Ability to exercise independent judgment and initiative within established guidelines, and to recognize when a matter should be escalated. Excellent verbal and written communication skills; ability to communicate clearly and professionally with faculty, staff, students, and offices across the University. Ability to foster positive working relationships and to coordinate effectively across academic and administrative units. Proficiency with standard office software and aptitude to learn and apply student-information systems, survey/registration tools, and related technology. Ability to handle confidential student information with discretion and in compliance with applicable policy and law (e.g., FERPA). Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Standard office and field setting. Duties performed are in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings. Duties are also performed in various outdoor settings, including high elevation mountains, deserts, and other potentially hazardous environments. The position requires working evenings, weekends, and some holidays. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly sit for long periods; walk short distances on a regular basis; travel to various locations to attend meetings and conduct work; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; stoop or kneel or crouch to file;  speak clearly and distinctly to answer telephones and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 20 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. BACKGROUND CHECK: Employment is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Offers are conditional and may be rescinded if disqualifying information is found or if information was withheld or falsified. Current employees may be affected if the check is not satisfactorily completed. DRIVER’S LICENSE: Certain positions require a valid driver’s license and enrollment in the DMV Government Employer Pull Notice Program to verify license status and driving record. PHYSICAL/PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: Select positions (e.g., Public Safety, Facilities, Student Affairs, Athletics, ITS) require a pre-employment physical exam, including the ability to lift 50+ lbs. Public Safety Officers must also pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. \nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=2a58a75e-e92a-4a40-8ef0-ab0ae7cdbd8b": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Theatre and Dance",
      "description": "Associate Professor of Theatre and Dance | Job Opportunities Skip to main content Accessibility Note: If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following page: Accessibility Accommodation for Applicants Dismiss Note Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Associate Professor of Theatre and Dance Job Category : Faculty Requisition Number : ASSOC001845 Apply now Posting Details Posted : September 22, 2026 Full-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: College of Arts & Science POSITION : Associate Professor of Theatre Appointment: Faculty SALARY RANGE: $79,000-$81,678 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. AVAILABLE: August 2027 POSTING DATE: September 17 , 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: November 1, 2026 TO APPLY: When applying, please upload the following materials; • letter of application • C.V. • two-page statement of teaching philosophy that includes a description of their approach to teaching students with diverse educational and socioeconomic backgrounds • Artistic Statement, Statement of Purpose, and/or portfolio In addition, applicants will send three letters of recommendation e-mailed to the Chair of the search committee, Gregory Ramos (Gregory_ramos@redlands.edu) Definition of Classification: The University of Redlands invites applications for a tenure-track Associate Professor who will serve as Chair of the Department of Theatre and Dance. Position starts August 2027. Seeking a theatre generalist with expertise in directing and costume design. Additional areas of interest include Acting, Dance, Musical Theatre, Theatre History, Chicano/Latine Theatre, Community based Theatre, Social Justice, Diversity in U.S. Theatre, and Devised Theatre. Responsibilities will include teaching the equivalent of three courses per semester, directing and designing productions and teaching courses that can be cross listed with Race and Ethnic Studies, Gender and Women’s Studies, and/or Studio Art. The Department of Theatre and Dance offers a BA in Theatre and Theatre Business as well as minors in Theatre, Musical Theatre, and Dance. Production and collegiality are central to the department’s approach to theatre education. As part of the School of Performing Arts the department works closely with the Conservatory of Music and produces 4-5 productions a year from large scale operas and musicals to smaller student driven projects. Performance spaces include a 334-seat proscenium stage, a 125-capacity black box theatre, and an intimate 50 seat space. As department chair, this position will be responsible for academic and administrative oversight, season planning in consultation with the Director of Production, marketing for productions, outreach to the community, and recruitment. The successful candidate will maintain a professional profile and participate in service to the university and the field. Candidate must hold an MFA or PhD with a demonstrated ability to serve as Department Chair. Deadline to apply: November 1, 2026. The University of Redlands is an independent, regional, comprehensive university with strong roots in liberal arts education. We are a Hispanic Serving Institution, with 40% of our student body composed of first-generation students and believe that bringing together faculty, students, and staff from diverse cultural and/or socio-economic backgrounds creates a richer learning environment. Furthermore, the University of Redlands is committed to providing students with transformative experiential learning opportunities through its Core Four initiative: undergraduate research, community service learning, internships, and study away. We welcome candidates who are enthusiastic about supporting student participation in high-impact practices and integrating experiential learning into their teaching, mentoring, and advising. Any combination equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Experience/Training/Education: The successful candidate will maintain a professional profile and participate in service to the university and the field. Candidate must hold an MFA or PhD with a demonstrated ability to serve as Department Chair. Compensation: Competitive salary commensurate with experience and qualifications. Application Process: letter of application V. two-page statement of teaching philosophy that includes a description of their approach to teaching students with diverse educational and socioeconomic backgrounds Artistic Statement, Statement of Purpose, and/or portfolio In addition, applicants will send three letters of recommendation e-mailed to the Chair of the search committee, Gregory Ramos ( Gregory_ramos@redlands.edu ) Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Standard office setting. Duties performed are primarily in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings. At least minimal environmental controls to assure health and comfort. This position requires flexibility to work evening and weekend hours as well as the ability to travel, as required. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly sit for long periods; walk short distances on a regular basis; may travel to various locations to attend meetings and conduct work; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; climb stairs; stoop or kneel or crouch to file; speak clearly and distinctly to answer telephones and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 20 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required, please additional request above Application Process. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. BACKGROUND CHECK : Employment is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Offers are conditional and may be rescinded if disqualifying information is found or if information was withheld or falsified. Current employees may be affected if the check is not satisfactorily completed. DRIVER’S LICENSE : Certain positions require a valid driver’s license and enrollment in the DMV Government Employer Pull Notice Program to verify license status and driving record. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Required Masters or better in Art or related field. PhD or better in Art or related field. Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. \nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=0eda1e72-6db0-4cd9-97a2-c24361cf920c": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Prospect Research and Management",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Prospect Research and Management | Job Opportunities Skip to main content Accessibility Note: If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following page: Accessibility Accommodation for Applicants Dismiss Note Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Assistant Director of Prospect Research and Management Job Category : Admin Requisition Number : ASSIS001848 Apply now Posting Details Posted : September 25, 2026 Full-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description Position Code: 8048 DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: Office of Advancement POSITION : Administrative, Exempt (12 months), Full-time (40 Hours) SALARY RANGE: $70,304-70,304 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. INTRODUCTORY PERIOD: Twelve (12) Months AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 24 , 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: The Assistant Director of Prospect Research and Management (ADPRM) serves as a strategic partner to Advancement leadership by providing prospect research, wealth intelligence, portfolio analysis, and prospect management support to advance fundraising priorities and institutional goals. The ADPRM is responsible for identifying, researching, analyzing, and evaluating prospective donors using a variety of biographical, philanthropic, financial, and public information sources. This position works closely with gift officers, Advancement Operations, and departmental leadership to develop prospect pipelines, enhance fundraising strategies, and support major gift cultivation and solicitation efforts. The ADPRM helps ensure that Advancement staff have access to accurate, timely, and actionable prospect intelligence to support effective donor engagement and fundraising success. Duties and Responsibilities: This position serves as a key resource within Advancement Operations by providing comprehensive prospect research, portfolio management support, wealth screening analysis, and fundraising intelligence. The role works closely with Advancement leadership, gift officers, and Advancement Services staff to identify new major gift prospects, assess philanthropic capacity, evaluate donor engagement, and support strategic fundraising initiatives. Responsibilities include conducting in-depth research and analysis, preparing prospect profiles and briefings, managing prospect pipelines, and monitoring fundraising portfolios to maximize development officer effectiveness. The position also supports prospect management processes, develops data-driven recommendations, and helps maintain research standards, confidentiality, and compliance with ethical research practices and institutional data governance policies. The duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following: Prospect Research and Analysis: • Conduct comprehensive research and analysis on individuals, corporations, foundations, and organizations to assess philanthropic capacity, affinity, and inclination to support the institution. • Prepare detailed prospect profiles, briefing materials, relationship maps, and research reports for Advancement leadership, gift officers, and institutional executives. • Identify and qualify new major gift, principal gift, and planned giving prospects through proactive research and data analysis. • Utilize public records, subscription research tools, wealth screening resources, and internal systems to gather and evaluate prospect information. • Support wealth screening projects and assist in evaluating screening results and recommendations. • Maintain current knowledge of wealth indicators, philanthropic giving patterns, and prospect research best practices. • Promote ethical research practices and ensure compliance with institutional policies and professional prospect research standards. • Ensure prospect research records are accurate, complete, and appropriately maintained within the CRM. Prospect Management: • Partner with development officers and Advancement leadership to develop and maintain prospect portfolios. • Conduct regular portfolio reviews to reflect pipeline movement and ensure updates are captured in the CRM. • Assist with the development and maintenance of prospect management policies, procedures, and business processes. • Collaborate with Advancement staff to ensure prospect records accurately reflect current research findings and engagement activity. Reporting and Analytics: • Maintain reports and dashboards related to prospect identification, portfolio performance, fundraising activity, and pipeline metrics. • Assist the Sr. Dir. of Advancement Operations & Prospect Research in monitoring key performance indicators related to major gifts and fundraising outcomes. • Coordinate recurring prospect and fundraising performance reports for Advancement leadership and frontline fundraising staff. • Performs other duties as assigned by the Sr. Director of Advancement Operations and Prospect Research. Qualifications Guidelines: Any combination of experience/training/education equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Required • Bachelor's degree preferred; high school diploma required with 1-3 years of related experience. • Demonstrated ability to gather, analyze, interpret, and present complex information from multiple sources. • Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and maintain confidentiality. Preferred • Experience in prospect research, wealth screening, prospect management, fundraising analytics, or related Advancement functions preferred. • Experience with prospect research resources, wealth screening tools, and fundraising databases preferred. • Knowledge of Slate for Advancement, DonorSearch, LexisNexis, or similar research platforms preferred. • Knowledge of APRA ethical standards and prospect research best practices preferred. Knowledge and Skills • Understanding of higher education and/or nonprofit fundraising environments, particularly Advancement and Development operations. • Strong analytical, critical thinking, and investigative research skills. • Experience with reporting tools, spreadsheets, and data analysis applications. • Advanced written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. • Effective project management skills and ability to analyze and document business processes. Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Duties performed are primarily in an office environment while sitting or standing at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings. Health and comfort prioritized. This position requires flexible working hours, which may include working some evenings, weekends, and some holidays. Physical Demands: This position requires sitting for long periods of time, as well as some bending and stooping when working with files. This position does have some limited lifting involved but not more than 10 pounds at a time. Quite a bit of finger/hand dexterity is involved regarding typing and/or filing. The overall setting of the job is in an area conducive to a normal office environment with minimal exposure to adverse conditions. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. BACKGROUND CHECK: Employment is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Offers are conditional and may be rescinded if disqualifying information is found or if information was withheld or falsified. Current employees may be affected if the check is not satisfactorily completed. DRIVER’S LICENSE: Certain positions require a valid driver’s license and enrollment in the DMV Government Employer Pull Notice Program to verify license status and driving record. PHYSICAL/PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: Select positions (e.g., Public Safety, Facilities, Student Affairs, Athletics, ITS) require a pre-employment physical exam, including the ability to lift 50+ lbs. Public Safety Officers must also pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws.\nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=7bfe4890-c871-4eb2-9486-b481b7e6ac38": {
      "title": "Public Safety Dispatcher",
      "description": "Public Safety Dispatcher | Job Opportunities Skip to main content Accessibility Note: If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following page: Accessibility Accommodation for Applicants Dismiss Note Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Public Safety Dispatcher Job Category : Staff Requisition Number : PUBLI001851 Apply now Posting Details Posted : September 29, 2026 Part-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description POSITION CODE: 7908,8056 DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: Office of Public Safety POSITION : Temp, Non-Exempt (3 months), Part-time (20 Hours) SALARY RANGE: $18.00-$20.00 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. INTRODUCTORY PERIOD: Twelve (12) Months AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 29, 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: The Public Safety Dispatcher receives, assimilates, and dispenses calls received for service from various locations on campus to on-duty Public Safety Officers. Serving as the first point of contact, provides excellent customer service, responding professionally, courteously, and empathetically to persons in various states of mind. This position reports to the Public Safety Sergeant. Duties and Responsibilities: The duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following: Answers departmental telephones assisting public and dispatching gathered information from calls (via microphone) to Public Safety Officers and communicating by telephone to the Redlands Police Department and Redlands Fire Department, when needed. Enters activity logs, parking permits and citations, into computer along with entering and posting of campus events schedule. Locates and relays information requested by officers; contacts emergency personnel when needed. Completes work orders as requested; receives and assimilates calls for repairs along with contacting the alarm company regarding alarm activation. Issues keys and parking permits after regular business hours. Adheres to all safety and/or housekeeping policies and procedures. Contributes to Team effort by accomplishing related duties as needed. Performs other duties and special projects as assigned. Qualification Guidelines Any combination equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Experience/Training/Education: Required High school diploma or equivalent supplemented with one year of course work in public relations or dispatching. One-year related experience in a similar position. Completion of a POST Public Safety Dispatcher Course is desirable. A comprehensive background check is required. Knowledge and Skills: Working knowledge of legal codes, requirements, procedures, and techniques for receiving complaints and calls for service. Basic knowledge of applicable automated dispatch and Public Safety systems and databases. Basic Knowledge of applicable federal and state laws and regulations. Interpret, apply, explain, and ensure compliance with applicable Federal, State, and local policies, procedures, laws, and regulations. Ability to independently respond and act quickly, accurately evaluate information and situations, and make appropriate decisions in routine, non-routine, and emergency situations. Working knowledge of geographical layout and ability to read maps and floor plans to provide directions to officers in the field. Considerable independent judgment, initiative, calm and understanding in stressful situations exercised in interpreting orders, rules, and regulations in emergency situations with the public. Excellent verbal and written communication skills to effectively communicate with diverse populations. Prepare clear and accurate reports, records, and other written materials. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships, interact with all members of the campus community and public, and maintain composure in highly stressful situations or when dealing with difficult individuals. Ability to maintain the confidentiality of sensitive information. Must be able to competently interact with a culturally and ethnically diverse population of students, faculty, and staff. Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Standard dispatch center office setting. Duties performed in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings. At least minimal environmental controls to assure health and comfort. The position may require after-hours and emergency response. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly sit for long periods; walk short distances on a regular basis; may travel to various locations to attend meetings and conduct work; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; stoop or kneel or crouch to file; speak clearly and distinctly to answer calls and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 10 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. The University of Redlands is required to provide a reasonable estimate of the compensation range for this role. This range considers the wide range of factors that are considered in making compensation decisions including but not limited to experience, skills, knowledge, abilities, education, licensure and certifications, and other business and organizational needs. It is not typical for an individual to be offered a salary at or near the top of the range for a position. Salary offers are determined based on final candidate qualifications and experience. The full salary range for this position is defined as the Full Pay Range. The budgeted salary or hourly range that the University reasonably expects to pay for this position is defined as the Hiring Range . Placement within the Hiring Range is determined by internal equity, and relevant qualifications. BACKGROUND CHECK: Satisfactory completion of a background check (including a criminal records check) is required for employment. The University will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background check reveals disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background check may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position. DRIVER'S LICENSE CHECK: Possession of a valid Driver's License is required for certain positions. Employees in positions that require a valid Driver’s License will be enrolled in the Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Government Employer Pull Notice Program which confirms possession of a valid driver's license and reflects driving record. PHYSICAL AND PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: For certain positions typically in Public Safety, Facilities, Athletics and Information Technology Services departments, employment is contingent upon a candidate passing a pre-employment physical exam with the ability to lift in excess of 50 lbs. Public Safety Officers, in addition to a pre-employment physical exam, will also be required to pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. For further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws.\nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=8c5aa6ce-3a80-4363-ae04-334e996b8fdb": {
      "title": "Legal Secretary / Paralegal",
      "description": "Legal Secretary / Paralegal | Job Opportunities Skip to main content Accessibility Note: If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following page: Accessibility Accommodation for Applicants Dismiss Note Toggle Navigation Find Opportunities U.S. English British English CA English Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Italian Português do Brasil Swedish U.S. English 简体中文 Sign In Post Legal Secretary / Paralegal Job Category : Admin Requisition Number : LEGAL001849 Apply now Posting Details Posted : September 29, 2026 Full-Time On-site Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations Job Details Description Position Code: 8054 DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: Office of General Counsel POSITION : Administrative, Exempt (12 months), Full-time (40 Hours) SALARY RANGE: $70,304-72,500 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. INTRODUCTORY PERIOD: Twelve (12) Months AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 29 , 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: Reporting to the Vice President for Institutional Integrity and Strategy & General Counsel, this position provides confidential, provides legal secretarial assistance and paralegal support to the University's Office of the General Counsel. The position also provides administrative support to the Office of Equity & Title IX, which reports to the Vice President for Institutional Integrity. Duties and Responsibilities: The duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following: • Manages daily office duties for the Office of the General Counsel (phone, calendaring, file management, office visitor intake, etc.). • Supports attorneys with case management, calendaring of hearings, trials, court dates, depositions, etc. • Receives legal process servers and gathers information to respond to subpoenas and other requests for information. • Assembles documents and exhibits for court filings and recordings, hearings, arbitrations, mediations, depositions, or other engagements. • Conducts legal research on laws, regulations, and legal articles. • Drafts legal documents, such as pleadings, motions, and briefs. • Assists with case preparation and management. • Coordinates communication between attorney’s and other parties. • Reviews and summarizes legal documents. • Assists General Counsel in the development, refinement, and daily operations of file management systems for invoices, cases, and contracts. • Opens new matters, files, and coordinates office workflow.• Assists in coordinating the activities of the Office of the General Counsel with other departments within the university. • Consistent with qualifications, may assist with legal research and other legal tasks such as drafting and preparing contracts for legal review and preparing summaries of court decisions and other material may assist with factual investigations pertaining to matters affecting the University may help organize the data and evidence obtained in such investigations may help draft proposed policies and work collaboratively with key leaders to seek the adoption of such policies. • Meet state continuing legal education requirements. • Responsible for daily electronic timekeeping as it applies to the scope of this position. • Performs other related duties and special projects as assigned. Qualifications Guidelines: Any combination of experience/training/education equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Required Bachelor’s degree in paralegal studies or related field; or any equivalent combination of education, training, and experience, which provides the required knowledge, skills, and abilities required. A certificate of completion of an ABA-approved paralegal program or equivalent statutorily required paralegal experience (Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code section 6450(c)). Five (5) years of experience in a law firm or in-house legal department as an officially designated paralegal, notary preferred. Experience in a variety of areas of law practice support, including case file management, legal research, legal forms, billing, contract management, processing responses to subpoenas, and drafting legal correspondence. Proficient in Microsoft Office suite products and Google Applications. Experience with Lexis-Nexis research tools. Knowledge and Skills Manage several projects simultaneously and make sound decisions. Work well with deadline pressures and distractions. High initiative with the ability to work independently. Ability to exercise confidentiality, good judgment, and discretion in job performance. Ability to quickly adapt to changing circumstances. Excellent organizational skills. Strong written communications skills. Interpersonal and trust-building skills. Analytical and problem-solving skills. Ability to take direction, work collegially in a diverse, complex, fast-paced, and highly professional environment. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, both on the phone and in-person, to interact with a broad and diverse range of leadership effectively and appropriately, faculty, staff. Ability to exercise good judgment and discretionary skills in determining when to act independently and when to consult management prior to taking action. This position interacts occasionally with senior level administrators. Able to learn procedures and retain information. Proficient in Microsoft Office suite software. High degree of accuracy. Attention to detail. Ability to handle time-sensitive projects while organizing and prioritizing work in a fast- paced environment, with minimal supervision. Must be able to competently interact with a culturally and ethnically diverse population of students, faculty, and staff. Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Standard office setting. Duties performed are primarily in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings. At least minimal environmental controls to assure health and comfort. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly sit for long periods; walk short distances on a regular basis; may travel to various locations to attend meetings and conduct work; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; climb stairs; stoop or kneel or crouch to file; must be able to climb stairs several times a day; speak clearly and distinctly to answer telephones and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 20 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. BACKGROUND CHECK: Employment is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Offers are conditional and may be rescinded if disqualifying information is found or if information was withheld or falsified. Current employees may be affected if the check is not satisfactorily completed. DRIVER’S LICENSE: Certain positions require a valid driver’s license and enrollment in the DMV Government Employer Pull Notice Program to verify license status and driving record. PHYSICAL/PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: Select positions (e.g., Public Safety, Facilities, Student Affairs, Athletics, ITS) require a pre-employment physical exam, including the ability to lift 50+ lbs. Public Safety Officers must also pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Required Bachelors or better. Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws.\nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
